Under NC Medicaid, a person is generally eligible for CAP/DA services if they meet both the **general Medicaid coverage standards** and the **CAP/DA-specific waiver criteria**.

## General CAP/DA eligibility criteria

A beneficiary must:

- **Meet nursing facility level of care (LOC) requirements** for HCBS, as determined by NC Medicaid, and need long-term care support at a level typically provided in an institution, tied to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
- **Have a CAP assessment showing a reasonable indication of need** for at least one CAP/DA home- and community-based service within 30 calendar days of the evaluation;
- **Have a signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services to be provided monthly, with coordinated case management to prevent institutionalization;
- If using short-term equipment, modification, or technology instead of ongoing monthly waiver services, **have a signed service plan authorizing that installation** to allow the person to return to or remain in a primary private residence and avoid institutional placement, with installation completed within three calendar months of service plan approval;
- Be **able to maintain health, safety, and well-being** in the primary private residence or another approved location using formal and informal supports;
- Have **paid care needs that can be maintained within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
- Have a **primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- Have an **emergency back-up and disaster plan** specifying who will provide care if key staff are unavailable, the primary caregiver has an emergency, or there is a public health emergency.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.2.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025.

## General Medicaid coverage standards that also apply

CAP/DA services also must satisfy NC Medicaid’s general coverage rules, meaning the service must be:

- **Individualized, specific, and consistent** with the person’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis, and not more than the person needs;
- **Safely furnished**, with no equally effective, more conservative, or less costly statewide alternative available; and
- **Not primarily for the convenience** of the beneficiary, caretaker, or provider.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 3.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025.

## When CAP/DA would not be covered

The policy also says CAP/DA is not covered if the beneficiary does not meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0 or the criteria in Section 3.0, or if the requested service is duplicative or experimental/investigational.  
[Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 4.1, effective Sept. 1, 2025.

## Bottom line

In plain terms, CAP/DA is for a Medicaid beneficiary who **would otherwise need institutional-level care**, **needs waiver services soon**, **has an approved service plan to keep them safely at home**, **can be served safely in the community**, and **can be served within waiver cost limits**. I checked amendments, and the current authoritative document is the Sept. 1, 2025 version of Policy 3K-2.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, outpatient therapy services must have a **treatment plan (plan of care)** that includes all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved within the duration of the therapy plan**;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the treatment plan. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 3.2.1.6 (effective 2025-02-01; current per amendment check).

## Documentation requirement
In addition to having the plan, the provider’s records must contain **a copy of the treatment plan** as part of the documentation for services. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) § 7.2 (effective 2025-02-01; current per amendment check).

## Practical takeaway
So, the rule is not just that a plan of care must exist. It must be:
- **written and signed**,
- **dated no later than the plan start date**,
- **time-limited to six months or less**, and
- detailed enough to show the **diagnosis, potential, measurable goals, interventions, frequency, and visit length**.

If you want, I can also pull together the related **order requirement** and **prior authorization implications** under Policy 10A.

Yes. A CAP/DA beneficiary can transfer, and the governing rule is the CAP/DA policy’s transfer provision in § 3.3. That section requires the Case Management Entity (CME) to coordinate the transfer so there are no gaps in services. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) (effective Sept. 1, 2025; current per amendment check).

## Rules governing transfers of eligible beneficiaries

Under **§ 3.3, “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries,”** before a transfer occurs, the CME must complete these steps:

- determine the anticipated service start date;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange a home visit within **5 business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence so the receiving entity can assess the home environment, identify health and welfare concerns, and plan mitigation/safety; and
- coordinate services so they begin on the **first date of transfer**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## If the beneficiary is transferring to a different county

Section 3.3 gives more specific rules for a **CAP/DA beneficiary transferring to a different county**:

- the transferring CME and receiving CME must coordinate the transfer on the agreed transfer date or **within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both agencies must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record must be transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving CME must arrange the home visit within **5 business days** of the move; and
- services must be coordinated to start on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Choice of provider

The CAP/DA service plan requirements also protect the beneficiary’s ability to choose providers. Section **5.4.2** requires the service plan to document the authorized provider and to ensure the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers** and among Medicaid-enrolled providers, evidenced by a signed freedom-of-choice form. That supports beneficiary transfers between service providers, subject to the coordination requirements above. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Federal authority

I could not verify from the retrieved text that **42 C.F.R. § 413.64** governs beneficiary transfers between CAP/DA service providers. The retrieved federal section addresses **payments to providers** on a reasonable cost/interim payment basis, not transfer procedures for CAP/DA beneficiaries. [42 CFR Part 413](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-413) (current per amendment check).

## Bottom line

A CAP/DA beneficiary **may transfer**, including when moving to another county or changing participating agencies, but the transfer must be coordinated by the CME under **CAP/DA § 3.3** to avoid service gaps, protect health and safety, transfer records, complete a timely home visit, and start services on the transfer date. The beneficiary’s provider choice is reinforced by **§ 5.4.2**.

Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to an identified beneficiary need and included in the beneficiary’s plan of care/service planning. The beneficiary also agrees to work collaboratively with the Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) to identify needs for “equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.” Current policy authority is **3K-2, Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA), effective Sept. 1, 2025**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

### Baseline coverage criteria
CAP/DA’s equipment, modification, and technology service must be used to:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve integration into the community;
- improve the beneficiary’s environmental and community accessibility; or
- address 24-hours-a-day, 7-days-a-week coverage concerns.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 3.4.5

The policy also places the service within the beneficiary’s collaborative planning obligations: the beneficiary agrees to work with the MDT to identify needs for modifications and related supports. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) “I agree to:”

### Residence/setting requirements
Home modifications are allowed for:

- a primary private residence where the beneficiary lives and that is owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence when the modifications are portable; or
- a rented residence when portable modifications are allowed by the owner.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY”

### Examples of covered home modifications
The policy lists examples that include:

- ramps;
- grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widened doorways/passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- emergency egress when medically necessary due to physical limitations of the responsible party;
- bathroom modifications;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen accessibility modifications;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- non-skid surfaces;
- door handle and door modifications;
- lifts and lift systems used primarily inside the home;
- certain smart home devices when the beneficiary lives alone and the need is identified in the assessment and person-centered service plan; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space when removed, dismantled, or damaged during an approved modification.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY”

### Related service components
CAP/DA may also cover related components of an approved modification, such as:

- technical assistance in device selection;
- training in device use by a qualified assistive technology professional;
- purchase, permits, inspections, taxes, and delivery charges;
- installation;
- assessment of the modification by the case manager and applicable inspectors to verify safety and that it meets the beneficiary’s needs; and
- repairs, if cheaper than replacement and not covered by warranty.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY”

### Limits and exclusions
The policy says CAP/DA does **not** cover, among other things:

- items not of direct medical or remedial benefit to the beneficiary;
- items covered under the Medicaid State Plan DME/home health benefit;
- items that are recreational in nature;
- items of general utility to a non-disabled person;
- warranties and unrelated service agreements/maintenance contracts; and
- certain other excluded equipment or monitoring systems not approved in the service plan.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) “Limits, Amount And Frequency”

## Documentation the MDT must provide

For equipment/home modification requests, the policy requires documentation including:

- **an MDT recommendation** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested assistive technology, equipment, or home modification;
- the recommendation must **state the cost** of the item the beneficiary requires;
- **a plan for training** the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment, with completion documented by the case manager and signed by the beneficiary or responsible party;
- **a statement of need** identified in the service plan;
- **evidence of medical need** recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- **itemized shipping costs** in the proposal request;
- any **other information required** for the specific item requested;
- when quotes are required for the purchase, adaptation, or modification, the number of quotes is determined by NC Medicaid; and
- in special circumstances, NC Medicaid or its designee determines what professionals must provide written recommendations.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) § 5.9.1

Supporting documentation requirements in the policy also include:

- a **comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration of need assessment** completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a **copy of the physician’s order**, when applicable;
- a **recommendation by an appropriate professional** identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the **estimated life of the equipment** and the expected length of time the beneficiary will benefit from it; and
- a supplier **invoice** showing the date provided and cost, with related charges maintained in the CAP Business system.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) “Limits, Amount And Frequency”

## Bottom line
For a CAP/DA home modification to be approved, it must be connected to the beneficiary’s assessed needs, fit within the service’s functional purposes, and generally be reflected in the service plan/person-centered planning. The MDT’s documentation must do more than simply request the modification: it must identify the need, support medical necessity where applicable, document cost and training, and include the required assessment and related professional/physician support.

NC Medicaid **does cover ambulance services only when the beneficiary meets the policy’s coverage criteria**, including emergency services needed to screen and stabilize the beneficiary. Emergency ambulance services do **not** require prior authorization if a prudent layperson would reasonably believe an emergency medical condition existed. If those coverage criteria are not met, the service is not covered. [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) (Policy 15, §§ 3.2, 3.2.1, effective 12/15/2023; current per amendment check)

## Ambulance services NC Medicaid does **not** cover

### General non-coverage rules
Ambulance services are not covered when:
- the beneficiary does **not** meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does **not** meet the criteria in Section 3.0;
- the service duplicates another provider’s service; or
- the service is experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial.  
[Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) § 4.1

### Specific non-covered ambulance situations
NC Medicaid does **not** cover:
- **Loaded mileage to a facility that is not the nearest appropriate facility**;
- transport based only on a physician’s staff privileges at a particular hospital;
- transport based only on the beneficiary’s facility preference.  
[Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) § 4.2.1

### Other non-covered ambulance services
NC Medicaid also does **not** cover:
- when **an ambulance is called and no treatment is needed**;
- **false alarm** ambulance responses;
- when **the beneficiary refuses all medical services**;
- ambulance transport for a **medical service that is not Medicaid-covered**;
- **commercial airline tickets**;
- **airstrip fees**;
- **taxes** charged separately (local, state, federal, etc.);
- separate additional charges for **nursing personnel** who are employees of a facility or ambulance service;
- **waiting fees**;
- separate charges for **oxygen and other items/supplies**, because those are included in the base rate and are not separately reimbursable;
- **services other than those listed in Section 3.2**.  
[Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) §§ 4.2.1, 4.2.1.4

## Bottom line
The clearest answer is that NC Medicaid does **not** cover ambulance services that fall outside the policy’s covered criteria, and it specifically excludes no-treatment calls, false alarms, refused-service calls, non-covered-destination or non-covered-purpose transports, excess mileage to a non-nearest appropriate facility, and various separate add-on charges like waiting fees, taxes, and separately billed oxygen/supplies. [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment)

