Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet the certification standards in Policy 12B and continue meeting them to stay certified. A provider can be decertified for a wide range of compliance, staffing, documentation, billing, and program-integrity failures. The current document I relied on is [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (effective Jan. 15, 2020), which the amendment record identifies as the authoritative current document. I also reviewed the current [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf).

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid requires an HIV case management provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure services are delivered by **qualified case managers** meeting the qualifications in Policy 12B;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting the policy’s supervisor standards;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- meet all **applicable state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary, operational, and financial records**; and
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed ownership, name, address, phone, or dissolution changes **within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than five business days after the actual change**. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1.

In addition, **within one year of Medicaid enrollment**, the provider must achieve **national accreditation** from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy, including ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1.

## What recertification materials show NC Medicaid expects

The recertification checklist confirms that NC Medicaid expects the provider’s submission to show, among other things:

- the application is for **one physical location**;
- a **signed recertification checklist** is included;
- all **case managers and supervisors** are listed with employment dates;
- all **counties served** are listed;
- all **services provided** are listed;
- **operating hours** and an **after-hours emergency plan** are included;
- the **accrediting agency name and dates** or an explanation are provided;
- the **frequency of satisfaction surveys** and use of results are described; and
- the numbers of **Medicaid and Ryan White clients** are listed. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf).

## When a provider can be decertified

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV case management provider for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide the required **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** for cited unmet program requirements within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the criteria in the policy;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units (CEUs)**;
- failure to provide staff **supervision** sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within the policy or Medicaid-designated timeframes;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing, including the documentation required by the policy;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely report agency changes in name, director, ownership, mailing address, or phone number, resulting in inability of NC Medicaid or its contractor to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with **applicable federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in § 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5.

## Practical bottom line

To be certified, an HIV case management provider must show organizational experience, qualified staff and supervisors, enrolled service sites, accreditation, compliance systems, record review access, and ongoing quality oversight. To avoid decertification, the provider must keep those standards in place and avoid problems involving staffing, training, documentation, billing integrity, confidentiality, corrective action, and notice of agency changes. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), §§ 6.2.1, 6.2.5; [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf).

